---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { site, contractMailto, conversationMailto } from '../data/site.ts';
import { careerStartYear } from '../data/experience.ts';

/**
 * The page written for the contract buyer, and the one artifact that can be
 * sent today.
 *
 * WHY IT EXISTS, given the rest of the site already says "available for
 * contract work" in three places.
 *
 * The primary route is a warm-network senior product-engineering contract
 * (livelihood/contracting.md), and the CV cannot be sent: it names a limited
 * company that will not exist until it is incorporated on an offer
 * (pipeline.md, Marketable row 1). So for the whole stretch between now and
 * an offer, the only artifact that can go in a recruiter's hand is a URL —
 * and the URL opened on the AI-native argument, which contracting.md itself
 * records as reading "he cannot code" to a sceptical hiring manager. The
 * meta description was demoted for exactly that reason on 2026-08-25; the
 * shape of the site still led with it.
 *
 * The other three surfaces each do a different job. The home page argues the
 * practice, /about/ is a memoir, /contact/ is a set of addresses. None of
 * them answers a recruiter's fifteen seconds or a hiring manager's one
 * paragraph, and every fact needed to was already written in livelihood and
 * had never reached a public surface — the proposition in three registers,
 * and the five facts from 2012 to 2020 a contract buyer actually wants, of
 * which the record notes that none currently lead anywhere public.
 *
 * FOUR THINGS ARE DELIBERATELY NOT ON THIS PAGE.
 *
 * 1. The day rate. £500 is a spoken instrument — one number, then silence
 *    (contracting.md § The rate). Published, it becomes a ceiling, forfeits
 *    the silence, and cannot be moved for a role worth more. "On request" is
 *    not evasion here; it is the negotiation staying where it works.
 * 2. Named references. Both are still to be asked for (Marketable row 2), and
 *    a page claiming "references available" before they are given is the one
 *    kind of overclaim this site cannot afford to make.
 * 3. Any counted figure. The agent-authorship share still carries an
 *    unverified definitions flag, `metrics.json` was last counted on
 *    2026-08-05 and is now behind a recount, and this is the page a reader
 *    checks hardest. Everything numerical is one click away in the case
 *    study, with its definition attached.
 * 4. The playbooks. `engineering-playbooks` is the artifact contracting.md
 *    names as the answer to "no public evidence of working alongside other
 *    engineers since 2020" — and it is still a private repository, so there
 *    is nothing to link. When it goes public, it belongs in § What you can
 *    look at below.
 *
 * THE AVAILABILITY SENTENCE IS VERBATIM AND CONTIGUOUS, and this page is the
 * sixth surface carrying it. The same rule that governs /contact/ and
 * /about/ governs it: no interpolation, no markup and no emphasis inside the
 * sentence, because livelihood's `text_matches_across` assertion normalizes
 * whitespace and Markdown and nothing else. It is listed in
 * livelihood/.groundtruth.jsonc in the same change that created this file —
 * a green gate over a partial list is worse than no gate, which that file
 * learned by committing the error itself on 2026-08-26.
 */

const startYear = careerStartYear();

/**
 * The recruiter block: everything asked on a first call, in the order it is
 * asked, answerable without reading a sentence of prose.
 *
 * "Incorporated on offer" is stated rather than smoothed over. The company
 * genuinely does not exist yet, saying otherwise on a public page is the
 * exact defect that keeps the CV in a drawer, and it happens to answer the
 * question an agency would have asked anyway.
 */
const termsBefore = [
  {
    term: 'Role',
    value: 'Senior full-stack engineer — TypeScript, Next.js, Node, Postgres.',
  },
  { term: 'Length', value: 'Three to six months, renewable.' },
  {
    term: 'Engagement',
    value:
      'Through my own UK limited company, incorporated on offer. Outside IR35 preferred; umbrella for inside-IR35 work.',
  },
  { term: 'Day rate', value: 'On request.' },
];

/**
 * Availability is rendered between these two arrays rather than as a row in
 * either, and the split is the whole reason there are two.
 *
 * It has to be one contiguous, uninterpolated, unmarked-up text node for the
 * reason in the header comment, so it cannot be a string in a `.map()` beside
 * the others without the risk that a later edit reaches for a `<strong>` or an
 * `{site.location}` and quietly breaks the assertion in livelihood. Keeping it
 * in the template makes that visible at the point of editing.
 *
 * It sits fourth because that is where it is asked on a first call — after the
 * rate and before the paperwork. Appending it to the end would have been the
 * implementation deciding the running order.
 */
const termsAfter = [
  {
    term: 'Based',
    value:
      'Bristol, UK. Remote-first — everything on this site was built and operated that way.',
  },
  {
    term: 'Right to work',
    value: 'British citizen. No visa or sponsorship needed in the UK.',
  },
  {
    term: 'Cover',
    value: 'Professional indemnity and public liability in place before the start date.',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Contract availability"
  description={`Jay Stewart is available for a three-to-six-month senior TypeScript, Next.js and Postgres contract — outside IR35 through his own limited company. Contracted for four years before a six-year break, and building production payments systems since ${startYear}. ${site.email}`}
>
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-content px-5 pt-16 pb-14 sm:px-8 sm:pt-24">
    <p class="eyebrow">Contract</p>
    <h1 class="mt-3 text-4xl sm:text-5xl">Available for a three-to-six-month contract.</h1>

    <p class="mt-6 text-lg text-muted">
      Senior TypeScript, Next.js and Postgres. I build and run production systems where money
      moves, and I have done since {startYear} — e-commerce backends including work on Dyson’s store,
      then a booking-and-payments platform I designed and kept running for four years as a contractor.
      Most useful where the hard part is the system rather than the screen, and where I would be reviewing
      other people’s code as well as writing my own.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-10 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
      <a href={contractMailto} class="btn btn-primary">{site.email}</a>
      <a href="/work/spiralclass/" class="btn btn-secondary">See what I built</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="terms-heading" class="border-y border-line bg-band py-14 sm:py-16">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-content px-5 sm:px-8">
      <h2 id="terms-heading" class="text-2xl">The short version</h2>

      <dl class="mt-8 space-y-5">
        {
          termsBefore.map((fact) => (
            <div class="grid gap-1 sm:grid-cols-[9rem_1fr] sm:gap-x-8">
              <dt class="eyebrow sm:pt-1">{fact.term}</dt>
              <dd class="text-muted">{fact.value}</dd>
            </div>
          ))
        }

        {/* Verbatim and contiguous — see the note above `termsAfter`. */}
        <div class="grid gap-1 sm:grid-cols-[9rem_1fr] sm:gap-x-8">
          <dt class="eyebrow sm:pt-1">Availability</dt>
          <dd class="text-muted">
            UK-based and available now. Currently on a temporary working trip abroad, with a
            daily overlap from about 13:00 UK and flexibility outside it. Back in the UK early
            in the engagement, and on site from then.
          </dd>
        </div>

        {
          termsAfter.map((fact) => (
            <div class="grid gap-1 sm:grid-cols-[9rem_1fr] sm:gap-x-8">
              <dt class="eyebrow sm:pt-1">{fact.term}</dt>
              <dd class="text-muted">{fact.value}</dd>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-content px-5 py-16 sm:px-8">
    <h2 class="text-3xl">This is a return, not a first attempt.</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-muted">
      The part of the record that matters to a contract client is the part furthest from the top
      of my CV, so it is here rather than at the bottom of a page about me.
    </p>

    <ul class="mt-10 space-y-8">
      <li>
        <h3 class="text-xl">I have contracted before, for four years.</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-muted">
          Two concurrent engagements in Bristol, both on day rate, both direct with the client:
          a booking-and-payments platform for a chiropractic clinic, and backend work at a
          venture-brand agency. Both ended in March 2020. What is new this time is the machinery
          rather than the work — I ran those self-employed, and a limited company is what the
          market runs on now.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3 class="text-xl">I kept one client for four years.</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-muted">
          I designed and built the clinic’s scheduling and card processing in 2016 and then
          operated it until 2020, while the practice grew into it — including the reporting they
          actually ran the business on. Contract clients buy renewal rather than novelty, and
          that is the most useful thing I can tell you about how an engagement with me tends to
          go.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3 class="text-xl">Agency delivery discipline.</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-muted">
          Three years of fixed launch dates, client engineering teams and other people’s
          codebases — high-traffic stores processing real transactions and real customer data.
          An unglamorous place to learn that reliability is a feature, and the working
          environment of most contracts.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3 class="text-xl">The thread is money.</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-muted">
          Transactions and customer data at scale, then booking and payments, and now booking
          and payments on two rails with payouts that cross a border. One class of problem,
          worked since {startYear}, where being wrong has always cost somebody their actual
          money rather than a failed build.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="gap-heading" class="border-y border-line bg-band py-16">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-content px-5 sm:px-8">
      <h2 id="gap-heading" class="text-2xl">The six years off</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-muted">
        I stopped in March 2020 and did not work in software again until March 2026. Both
        contracts had ended and I did not go looking for more: I could afford not to, and after
        eight years of agency e-commerce I wanted to find out what else there was. That turned
        into six years of travelling.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-muted">
        I am not going to dress it up as a sabbatical with a thesis attached. I stopped because
        I wanted to and could, and I came back for the same reason. What brought me back was
        coding agents — the discovery that the bottleneck had moved, not the novelty. Everything
        on this site was built after March 2026.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-content px-5 py-16 sm:px-8">
    <h2 class="text-3xl">Where I am most useful, and where I am not.</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-muted">
      Ruling out the wrong engagement early is worth more to both of us than a call that
      discovers it in week two.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-10 grid gap-10 sm:grid-cols-2">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">A good fit</p>
        <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-muted">
          <li>
            Systems with real consequences — payments, scheduling, integrations, anything where
            a wrong state costs money or time rather than a rebuild.
          </li>
          <li>
            Teams who want the delivery pipeline as well as the code: a merge gate that has to
            be green, a coverage floor that only ratchets upward, migration-drift and deployment
            checks, and decision records that say why each exists.
          </li>
          <li>
            Reviewing other people’s code. Everything since March 2026 has been built by a team
            of one, and this is the part of a team I actually miss.
          </li>
          <li>
            Teams already building with agents, or about to be. I have run production systems
            that way since March and measured what it did. Day to day that is Claude Code and
            the agentic patterns around it &mdash; and the half worth hiring for is the
            verification that makes agent output safe to merge, not the generation.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Not a good fit</p>
        <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-muted">
          <li>
            Design-led frontend work. I work across the stack and write interfaces when the
            interface is what is in the way, but I am not the person to hand a design system to.
          </li>
          <li>
            PHP and Magento. Eight years of it are in my history and none of it is on offer —
            the stack I want to be held to is the one I have shipped this year.
          </li>
          <li>
            A synchronous culture with a 09:30 stand-up, until I am back in the UK. The overlap
            starts about 13:00 and that is a real constraint rather than a soft one. An
            asynchronous-friendly team will not notice it; a team that lives in the morning
            will.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="evidence-heading" class="border-y border-line bg-band py-16">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-content px-5 sm:px-8">
      <h2 id="evidence-heading" class="text-3xl">What you can look at</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-muted">
        Three of these are open to a stranger with no introduction from me, which is the only
        reason they are worth listing.
      </p>

      <dl class="mt-10 space-y-7">
        <div>
          <dt class="text-lg">
            <a href="https://spiralclass.com" rel="noopener" class="link-underline">
              SpiralClass, live ↗
            </a>
          </dt>
          <dd class="mt-2 text-muted">
            Booking, package purchases, subscriptions, payouts that cross a border, and live
            classes with translated captions. In production since May 2026, carrying real
            bookings and real money. You can open a teacher’s public booking page and walk the
            checkout without credentials.{' '}
            <a href="/work/spiralclass/" class="link-underline">The engineering write-up</a> has the
            trade-offs and the mistakes intact.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="text-lg">
            <a href="https://groundtruth.sh" rel="noopener" class="link-underline"
              >groundtruth ↗</a
            >
          </dt>
          <dd class="mt-2 text-muted">
            A small open-source CLI and GitHub Action that fails CI when an agent-context file
            makes a claim the repository no longer supports. MIT, on npm, and it gates this
            site’s own pull requests. It exists because{' '}
            <a href="/notes/a-compiler-for-agent-context/" class="link-underline">
              auditing my own context layer
            </a>{' '}
            turned up claims that had quietly gone false.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="text-lg">
            <a href={`${site.github}/jaystewart.co.uk`} rel="noopener" class="link-underline">
              This site’s source ↗
            </a>
          </dt>
          <dd class="mt-2 text-muted">
            Static Astro, no client framework, four required CI jobs including axe and
            Lighthouse budgets. Small, but it is the one thing here you can read end to end in
            an afternoon and judge for yourself.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="text-lg">The code you cannot see</dt>
          <dd class="mt-2 text-muted">
            SpiralClass is private and stays private — it holds real teachers’ and students’
            bookings, messages and payment records, so there is no browse-the-repository route
            into it and I would not offer one. I am happy to walk through any of it on a call:
            schema, failure modes, the decision log, the parts that went wrong first.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-content px-5 py-16 sm:px-8 sm:py-20">
    <h2 class="text-3xl">The next step is twenty minutes.</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-muted">
      If you have a role, tell me what the system does and where it hurts, and I will tell you
      straight away whether I am the right person for it. If you are a recruiter, everything
      above is what you need for a submission — ask me for the rest.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-muted">
      Separately, and with no role behind it: if your team builds with coding agents, I am still
      interested in what that actually changes inside a real engineering organization.{' '}
      <a href={conversationMailto} class="link-underline">Twenty minutes comparing notes</a> is welcome
      either way.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
      <a href={contractMailto} class="btn btn-primary">{site.email}</a>
      <a href="/contact/" class="btn btn-secondary">Other ways to reach me</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
